<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dawaro</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dawaro was a historic Muslim region and polity in the south-eastern highlands of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nam-tien">southward expansion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a> in the fourteenth century, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> and other Muslim sultanates. Its absorption formed part of the empire's medieval consolidation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Consolidation came under Amda Seyon I, who acceded in 1314, organised the army into standing chewa regiments, and expanded southward across the Awash River and eastward, subduing Ifat, <b>Dawaro</b>, Bale, and other Muslim principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The states of eastern Ethiopia that had preceded it, Gidaya, <b>Dawaro</b>, Sawans, Bali and Fatagar, were incorporated into the sultanate but kept a measure of independence; Taddesse Tamrat counts Fatagar, Dawaro and Bale within its borders, and Fatagar stayed under Ifat's control after the rest were invaded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dawaro</b> lay south of Ifat, between the Awash and the upper Wabi Shebelle river, and appears in sources from the 13th century as one of the Muslim states of the Ethiopian interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-dawaro">Sultanate of Dawaro</a></li></ul></section>
